    "summary": "Tight Utica set with soaring solos and epic Cassidy jam",
    "blurb": "The show rides a high‑energy wave from an opening Shakedown Street through tight jams on Cassidy and Althea. Jerry delivers expressive vocals and soaring guitar work, while Brent’s keyboards add color to ballads. Overall the performance is vibrant and consistently strong throughout both sets.",
    "review": "March 13, 1981 at Utica Memorial Auditorium finds the Dead in top form, launching the evening with a punchy \"Shakedown Street\" that immediately lifts the crowd. The first set builds momentum through bright renditions of \"The Music Never Stopped,\" \"Cassidy\" (which segues into an inspired \"Althea\") and a driving \"High Time.\" Jerry’s voice is noted as especially strong, and his solos on \"El Paso\" and \"They Love Each Other\" soar above the mix. Brent’s keyboards shine on the ballads and the funky transition from \"Shakedown\" into \"New Minglewood Blues\" adds a fresh groove.\nThe second set deepens the journey with a fluid \"Estimated Prophet → Eyes Of The World\" that blossoms into an expansive drum‑and‑space segment before moving through \"Lost Sailor,\" \"Wharf Rat\" and closing on a joyous \"Sugar Magnolia.\" Reviewers highlight the tight chemistry between Jerry and Bob, particularly during the extended \"Cassidy\" jam, while Mickey and Billy lock in a propulsive rhythm foundation throughout. The audience tapes captured by wise/lutch and other sources are remarkably clear for the era, making this night an excellent entry point for fans seeking both stellar playing and high‑quality listening.",
